{"name":"Velvet Leaf","hex":"#96c193","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"velvet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closely woven fabric (originally of silk, now also of cotton or man-made fibres) with a thick short pile on one side.","Very fine fur, including the skin and fur on a deer's antlers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with velvet or with a covering of a similar texture.","To coat raw meat in starch, then in oil, preparatory to frying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velvet"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Abutilon theophrasti","blocks":[{"text":"Abutilon theophrasti, also known as velvetleaf, velvet plant, velvetweed and the Chinese jute, is an annual plant in the family Malvaceae that is native to Central and East Asia. It is the type species of the genus Abutilon. Its specific epithet, theophrasti, commemorates the ancient Greek botanist-philosopher Theophrastus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Abutilon_theophrasti_sl27.jpg/330px-Abutilon_theophrasti_sl27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abutilon_theophrasti","wikidata":"Q606369"},{"title":"Velvet","blocks":[{"text":"Velvet is a type of woven fabric with a dense, even pile that gives it a distinctive soft feel. Historically, velvet was typically made from silk. Modern velvet can be made from silk, linen, cotton, wool, synthetic fibers, silk-cotton blends, or synthetic-natural fiber blends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet","wikidata":"Q243519"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Abutilon_th333.jpg/500px-Abutilon_th333.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Abutilon_th333.jpg/500px-Abutilon_th333.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flower and leaves","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbutilon_th333.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg/500px-Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg/1280px-Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg/500px-Abutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abutilon theophrasti - MHNT","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbutilon_theophrasti_MHNT.BOT.2007.43.38.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG/500px-Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG/1280px-Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG/500px-Roz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schizocarp on a plant that grew in California.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoz%C5%82upnia_na_ro%C5%9Blinie_wyros%C5%82ej_w_Kalifornii.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Abutilon_theophrasti_seedling.jpg/500px-Abu